 In the Mishna the Hebrew word for “fastened” is “Tephurim,” literally meaning “sewed” or “embroidered”; 
i.e.
, the Totaphoth and Sarbitin as worn by the wealthy were ornaments made of gold or silver with inscriptions engraved on them, but the poor made them of various colored materials (as explained in the Gemara farther on) and embroidered the inscriptions on them. The prohibition of the Mishna therefore refers only to the wearing of such ornaments before the inscriptions were either engraved or embroidered on them. Such is our explanation in our History of Amulets,” pp. 11-15.

